SMARDDA-PFC software from UKAEA
-------------------------------
SMARDDA-PFC is the fieldline tracing kernel of SMITER = SMARDDA for ITER ;
for a description of the SMARDDA ray-tracing algorithm applied to fieldlines
see http://dx.doi.org/10.1109/TPS.2014.2320904 (also http://arxiv.org/abs/1403.7142 ).
SMARDDA-PFC uses SMARDDA-LIB which contains the SMARDDA ray-tracing kernel
see  http://dx.doi.org/10.1109/TPS.2015.2458897 (also http://arxiv.org/abs/1403.6750 ).
The SMARDDA modules have been tested on CentOS 7.0, Ubuntu 22.04 and Red Hat 4.4 (Scientific Linux)
and are known to run on MacOS and WSL 1 and 2.
Try to use the latest stable fortran compiler version available.

*NOTE* you must first install the ray-tracing kernel SMARDDA-LIB,
which may be facilitated by download of git@github.com:smardda/bootstrap.git
that also helps with PFC.

To proceed to build from here (and only from here), type
export SMITER_DIR=$PWD
source ./exec/build intel
The argument to build which is optional, will cause the script to try to use the Intel fortran compiler, 
which is advisable on ITER hpc machines as a Fortran compiler release post-2011 is needed.
Note that this script
(1) recommends you source the file ./Extras/setup so that the main
SMARDDA-PFC executables are placed on your path.
(2) sets the shell variables SMITER_DIR and SMALIB_DIR; you may find it a good idea to ensure
that SMITER_DIR and SMALIB_DIR are always set by modifying your .bashrc file, by adding the lines,
for example
export SMITER_DIR=~/smardda/smardda-pfc
export SMALIB_DIR=~/smardda/smardda-lib
(3) takes an optional argument N stopping the production of documentation

bash shell release 3.2 or later (i.e. from 2005 or later) is needed.
ParaView may be downloaded separately from www.paraview.org if not already installed.

To produce the html documentation in $SMITER_DIR/doc, note
particularly that doxygen 1.8.6+ is needed.
(For creation of user manual as a pdf the texlive/2015 module is needed:
if it is installed as on freia, then the commands are:
module load texlive/2015
cd ./doc/srcdoc/latex/; pdflatex refman
else a version suitable for the target machine will have to be found and installed)

To run the test deck, to be found in separate directory smiteraux,
https://git.iter.org/projects/BND/repos/smiteraux/browse
orchestrated by file tests.bash, it is important to ensure the following
shell variable is set, for example
export HSAUX=~/smiteraux
Linux modules dated post-2011 are generally required.
The following module versions are indicative:
module load qt/4.8.7
module load paraview/4.4.0
module load GCC/4.8.3
module load dialog/1.2
module load flex
module load doxygen/1.8.8
module load gnuplot
module load convert
(others may be needed, and not all machines will have exactly these versions).
The test deck should be run before any problems are reported.

The script smpfcint guides the user interactively through the setting up of a basic test
case, starting with the sub-class of MSC Nastran-TM .dat files output by CATIA-TM.
smpfcint has a significant inbuilt help capability
For efficient smpfcint interaction with ParaView, the following settings are needed:
Settings->General, Click on gearwheel, untick Show Splash Screen, tick AutoApply (near top)
(Also useful is parallel projection:
[vtk file loaded] -> Properties tab, Click gearwheel, tick Camera Parallel projection (near bottom)
See SMARDDA-PFC user manual webpages for further details of (i)smiter use.
file://$SMITER_DIR/doc/srcdoc/html/index.html
(assuming that doxygen doxyfile has been executed correctly in  $SMITER_DIR/doc).
Note that the CADfix-TM interface between CATIA-TM and SMARDDA/SMARDDA-PFC is not supported by ITER.

The script tes.sgo in $SMITER_DIR/Extras show how SMARDDA-PFC works in detail, given the
necessary geometry in legacy vtk format. Detailed descriptions of the namelists in the .ctl
files (which control execution) may be found by pointing a browser at
file://$SMITER_DIR/doc/srcdoc/html/classes.html
(assuming that doxygen doxyfile has been executed correctly in  $SMITER_DIR/doc).

Developers should notice that the SMARDDA modules were developed according to the principles and
conventions set out in http://dx.doi.org/10.13140/RG.2.2.27018.41922} mirroring
https://scientific-publications.ukaea.uk/wp-content/uploads/CCFE-R-1534.pdf
Production of support files and scripts for developers has been initiated.
These may be accessed  from 
git@github.com:smardda/develop.git

